+## Tap to walk
+
+Touch the floor and Twinsen walks there. Keep the finger down and he follows it; lift it and he
+carries on to the last spot. A ring on the floor marks where he is heading, and stays up after the
+buttons have faded. The tank-style D-pad is still there if you prefer it (see the setting below).
+
+How it behaves, and why it is built that way, is written up in
+[`TAPMOVE.H`](../SOURCES/TAPMOVE.H). In short:
+
+- **A touch on empty glass is a walk order.** A touch that lands on a button is that button's. The
+  first touch after using another device only wakes the overlay, as everywhere else.
+- **It presses the keys a player would.** Each gameplay frame `TapMove_Steer` ORs the up / left /
+  right bits into the input the engine reads, right where the replay harness ORs its own.
+  Animations, speed, collisions, climbing, swimming and the behaviour modes are the engine's
+  existing movement code, untouched.
+- **The touch becomes a spot on the floor through the camera the frame was drawn with**, at the
+  height of the hero's feet: the engine's own world-to-screen projection is inverted, so it holds
+  for the classic isometric rooms and the perspective Auto camera alike.
+- **Interiors route round obstacles.** The route is found on the cube's brick grid (A\*,
+  eight-way moves, no corner cutting, straightened afterwards), so a tap on the far side of a wall
+  walks round it, and a spot that is walled off is approached as closely as it can be. Islands are
+  not on the brick grid: there he walks straight at the spot.
+- **It stops** on arrival; on a new tap; on any keyboard or gamepad movement; when the hero leaves
+  manual control (a cutscene, a script, death); when a dialogue, a menu or the inventory comes up;
+  after about twenty seconds; and when he is blocked: the first time the route is worked out again
+  from where he stands, the second time the order is dropped.
+- **It is off during replays and the scripted harness.** A recording already holds the keys this
+  produced; generating them again would drive the hero twice.
+
+The switch is `TouchTapToMove` in `lba2.cfg`, or `touch_tap_move` on the console
+(see [`CONFIG_FILE.CPP`](../SOURCES/CONFIG_FILE.CPP)): `1` (the default) for tap to walk, `0` for the
+on-screen D-pad as before.
+
+Limits worth knowing:
+
+- Routes are planned on the floor the hero is standing on. A spot on another level of a room (up a
+  staircase, on a platform) is found at his own height, not where it appears on screen, and a route
+  does not climb.
+- Things the brick grid does not contain (a character in the way, a block being pushed) are not
+  routed round; the walk is dropped after the re-plan finds it blocked again.
+- It has not been exercised on every scene: if a route goes somewhere odd, the D-pad setting is the
+  way out, and the scene is worth reporting.
+
 ## When the overlay shows
 
 It follows the last input device the player actually used, and fades when they
